@Marine Biotechnology for Biofuels is an emerging field that combines marine biology, biotechnology, microbiology, chemistry, and engineering to investigate renewable fuels derived from marine biological resources. Microalgae, macroalgae or seaweed, and marine microorganisms contain lipids, carbohydrates, proteins, and other organic compounds that can potentially be transformed into different forms of bioenergy.

Scientists are particularly interested in marine algae because some species grow rapidly, can use saline water, and do not necessarily require productive agricultural land. Depending on their biochemical composition and the conversion technology used, marine biomass can contribute to biodiesel, bioethanol, biogas, biohydrogen, bio-oil, and potentially advanced transportation fuels.

Research nevertheless shows an important distinction between technical potential and commercial readiness. Marine microalgal biofuels remain constrained by cultivation costs, harvesting, downstream processing, energy requirements, and difficulties in scaling laboratory technologies to industrial production.

@Marine Biotechnology for Energy is an emerging scientific field that uses marine organisms, biological processes, and biotechnology to investigate new ways of producing renewable fuels and energy products. Microalgae, macroalgae or seaweed, marine bacteria, and other microorganisms contain biological materials that can potentially be converted into biodiesel, biogas, bioethanol, biohydrogen, and other fuels.

Interest in these resources has increased because conventional fossil fuels release carbon dioxide that has been stored underground for millions of years, while biological energy systems can potentially operate within shorter carbon cycles. However, calling a fuel “bio-based” does not automatically make it low-carbon. Cultivation, harvesting, drying, processing, transportation, electricity use, nutrients, and conversion efficiency all influence its overall environmental performance.

Research reviews continue to identify algae as promising energy feedstocks, while also emphasizing substantial economic, engineering, and scale-up challenges.
